AI Clinical Trial Card: GPT-4 Assistance for Improvement of Physician Performance on Patient Care Tasks: A Randomized Controlled Trial

Authors: Eric Goh et al.

AI Model Details

Provider: OpenAI

Model Name and Version:

GPT-4 (exact version not specified)

Access Method:

GPT-4 accessed through a web-based chat interface

Participants could query the model during task completion

Fine-tuning:

No fine-tuning reported

Model used in a general-purpose configuration

Prompting / Configuration Parameters:

Participants were given standardized prompts describing how to consult GPT-4

No control over temperature or decoding parameters reported

Human Participant Profile

Sample Size:

48 physicians

Population / Expertise:

Practicing physicians

Highly trained domain experts with clinical experience

Domain Expertise:

Patient care and medical decision-making

AI / LLM Familiarity:

Not extensively characterized

Likely low-to-moderate familiarity given early GPT-4 adoption

Training / Onboarding:

Brief instructions on how to interact with the AI system

Interaction and Task Design

Task Description:

Physicians completed patient care tasks, including clinical reasoning and treatment decisions

Tasks designed to reflect realistic diagnostic and management scenarios

Interface:

Chat-based interface for interacting with GPT-4

AI Role / Degree of Autonomy:

Assistive

GPT-4 provided recommendations and reasoning support; final decisions remained with physicians

Experimental Conditions:

Control: no AI assistance

Treatment: GPT-4 assistance

Primary Outcome Measures:

Quality of clinical decisions, assessed using expert-defined criteria

Main Effects:

GPT-4 assistance significantly improved physician performance

Improvements observed despite participants’ high baseline expertise

Notes on Interpretation

Demonstrates that generative AI can meaningfully support expert decision-making in high-stakes domains

Effects depend on careful task framing and controlled use

Does not address downstream impacts on patient outcomes or long-term learning